# SYNOPSIS

```text
cpu -h node [-k key] [-c command ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

cpu runs rc(1) on another node, as Plan 9's cpu does: rc runs there, in that node's namespace, and its terminal is this one.
<node> is the other node's address, or a dial string (dial(2)); without a `!` it is `tcp!`<node>`!17019`, its cpu server's (cpu(8)).
<key> is that node's public key, 64 hex digits, which its cpu server prints as it starts: Noise IK (noise(2)) calls only the node holding it.
Without `-k`, <node> names a member of the swarm file, and its entry's `key` is the one (ndb(6)): `cpu -h board`.
With `-c` the rest of the line is rc's command; without it rc is interactive.

This node is the caller as itself: the handshake runs through factotum(4)'s `proto=noise-ik` key, the node's own, which a window's programs may use (rio(1), shell(8)) and never see.
The other node answers only a node whose key its image pins (cpu(8)); any other call gets no reply, and cpu says the handshake failed.

Once called, cpu serves this namespace, all of it, to the other node, which mounts it at `/mnt/term` and binds `/mnt/term/dev/cons` over rc's `/dev/cons`.
So rc's output is this window's and its input this window's lines, edited here, with no pty on either node.
The window's interrupt (Delete) is a note to cpu, which passes it to the other node's rc through `dev/cpunote`, a file it adds after this namespace's `/dev`, as vx-cpu(1) does from the dev machine; any other note ends cpu.
cpu ends when the session does: when rc ends there, or the connection goes.

# EXAMPLES

From a window on one node, a command on the board:

```rc
% cpu -h 192.168.0.24 -k d468c6c6… -c 'cat /dev/sdN0/ctl'
```

An rc there, which reads this node's files:

```rc
% cpu -h 192.168.0.24 -k d468c6c6…
% cat /mnt/term/lib/ndb/local
```

